    {
      "id": "g31p2ah",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Volcanoes"
    },
    {
      "id": "t200inx",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "p0jnhua",
      "src": "/books/Guide/SSC GS & GK GUIDE BOOK/images/image_p0jnhua.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Volcanoes are classified by activity into Active, Dormant, and Extinct types, and by structure into Shield, Composite, and Cinder Cone volcanoes.",
        "Earthquakes generate three main types of seismic waves: Primary (P) waves, Secondary (S) waves, and Surface (L) waves.",
        "The subterranean point of origin of an earthquake is the Focus (Hypocenter), while the point directly above it on the surface is the Epicenter.",
        "Earthquake magnitude is measured logarithmically on the Richter Scale, whereas intensity and damage are measured on the Mercalli Scale.",
        "The Pacific Ring of Fire is the most active seismic and volcanic zone in the world, encircling the Pacific Ocean basin."
      ],
      "id": "voezm7v"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pfidjw5",
      "content": "A volcano is an opening in the Earth's crust through which molten rock (magma), gases, and ash are expelled to the surface. When magma reaches the Earth's surface, it is called lava."
    },
    {
      "id": "8rnpsv9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Structure of a Volcano"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wmx8amv",
      "content": "• Crater – bowl-shaped opening at the top of a volcano."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lid8z6i",
      "content": "• Vent – the channel through which magma reaches the surface."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v4a4hde",
      "content": "• Magma Chamber – underground reservoir of molten rock."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m4qfdm8",
      "content": "• Lava Flow – molten rock flowing out during eruption."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fvvors6",
      "content": "• Ash and Gas – volcanic eruptions release ash, steam, and gases."
    },
    {
