    {
      "id": "9r1b7jh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Causes of Earthquakes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "csjcn4n",
      "content": "• Movement of tectonic plates."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y7ndx7b",
      "content": "• Volcanic activity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gztsw13",
      "content": "• Faulting in Earth's crust."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e355w5n",
      "content": "• Human activities such as mining and dam construction."
    },
    {
      "id": "4615i97",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Measurement of Earthquakes"
    },
    {
      "id": "jfvttwt",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Scale</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Richter Scale</p>",
            "<p>Measures magnitude (energy released).</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mercalli Scale</p>",
            "<p>Measures intensity based on damage observed.</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "e5nirj6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Effects of Earthquakes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f5a6rci",
      "content": "• Damage to buildings and infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8ghd3z7",
      "content": "• Landslides and ground cracks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b486ucp",
      "content": "• Tsunamis in coastal regions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tnk90gm",
      "content": "• Loss of life and property."
    },
    {
      "id": "8ws5mfz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Major Earthquake Zones of the World"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "P-waves (Primary waves) travel through solids, liquids, and gases, whereas S-waves (Secondary waves) can only pass through solid media.",
        "Focus is the actual point inside the Earth where energy is released, whereas Epicenter is the point on the Earth's surface directly above the focus.",
        "Richter Scale measures energy released (magnitude 1-10), while Mercalli Scale measures visible destruction and intensity (I-XII)."
      ],
      "id": "cuhyzj2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6vrcqao",
      "content": "• Pacific Ring of Fire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xc94mdx",
      "content": "• Mid-Atlantic Ridge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dmh0vw1",
      "content": "• Himalayan belt."
    },
    {
      "id": "eq61c92",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yvu3h8n",
      "content": "• Pacific Ring of Fire has the highest number of volcanoes and earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1oew89u",
      "content": "• P waves travel through solids and liquids; S waves travel only through solids."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "weha3dk",
      "content": "• Epicenter is directly above the earthquake focus."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t6g53if",
      "content": "• Richter scale measures magnitude of earthquakes."
    }
  ],
